  "interpretation": "## What changed\n\nOver 90 days, 2,714 people took 1,057,387 actions, and 64.23% came back to use it again. Weekly users peaked at 830 in mid-August and dropped to 339 in the latest week. Yet the estimated work in that week rose to 515,787, the highest in the history. This suggests a smaller group now does heavier work. In the latest 14 days, users rose 5.59%, while actions rose 36.58%. Release 2.0.8 reached 56.28% of weekly users in late September. The newest release, 2.3.7, reached 5.3% in its first week.\n\n## How people use it\n\nShare of work is an estimate, not time or speed. Quick reads count 1, everyday edits count 3, and heavy data work counts 10. Reading and writing cells made up 63.28% of actions but 48.17% of estimated work. Running macros was 8.01% of actions but 17.17% of work. Data imports were 4.08% of actions but 11.22% of work. Of people who used charts and summary tables, 76.53% tried them on their first day.\n\nIn the last 30 days, 67.09% of AI assistant sessions used more than one area. Cell work and sheet tasks were paired in 56.4% of sessions. The biggest sessions were only 4.82% of sessions but held 47.65% of actions. Of new people, 51.98% came back after a week. Workdays averaged 22,909 actions, and weekends 11,575. Typical waits were 2.62 seconds for data imports and 0.06 seconds for cell work. Waits depend on workbook size and the computer, so they are not a quality score.\n\nThe AI assistant and command line groups are different people, counted over a shorter recent window. AI assistant users averaged 188.5 actions each, and command line users 129.6. Data imports may matter more on the command line, at 19.26% of its estimated work.\n\n## Where people get it\n\nGitHub release files have 23,245 downloads, VS Code extension installs 8,739, npm MCP Server 2,152, and npm command line 1,572. npm totals cover the last 12 months; the others count since each channel started. In the latest npm week there were 2,891 downloads. Release 2.3.3 was downloaded most among recent releases, with 576, then 2.3.0 with 334. Downloads count downloads, not people. One person can download several times, updates and automated installs add to totals, and channels overlap, so they should not be added together. Weekly download history starts building from this report.\n\n## What we will improve\n\nThese are plans, not observations. We will make data imports and macros easier to follow, since they carry much of the estimated work. We will help new people find charts and data tools early. We will also encourage upgrades to the newest release.",
